> > Based on this, we can calculate the score as dot(d, normalize(o)) / length(o) which is equivalent to dot(d, o) / sqrLength(o)

<img src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7677617b-d05f-41cb-bd3d-b58b61719968" width="50%">

If I understand and calculate correctly, the scoring formula for any point $P$ is as follows:

$$
\begin{align}
score(P) = \frac{\cos{\angle{PAB}}}{\vec{|AP|}}
\end{align}
$$

The equal score line will be a circle. 

$$
\begin{align}
& \vec{|AB|} = L  \quad \angle{CAB}=\alpha \\
& \vec{|AC|} = L\cos{\alpha} \\
& score(B) = \frac{\cos{0}}{\vec{|AB|}} = \frac{1}{L} \\
& score(C) = \frac{\cos{\alpha}}{\vec{|AC|}} = \frac{\cos{\alpha}}{L\cos{\alpha}} =  \frac{1}{L}
\end{align}
$$

That is to say, the equal-score point of B should be on the circle with AB as the diameter.

This algorithm is suitable for actions that can be aimed (using axes), that is, it focuses on ensuring the correct direction, You need to aim for the center of the target control to navigate.

Godot currently mainly uses 4-directional navigation. The current equal score line in Godot is flat-topped. 
It expands forward at the same rate as it expands to the sides, **this is the key to the issue**. As shown in the following figure:

<img src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/0cebacb5-14ef-41f9-9a28-6acd2f830b01" width="50%">

In this case, maybe using bubble blowing algorithm might be a better approach. The diagram is as follows:

<img src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a3e37011-45b3-47bc-a83b-846f2571dca7" width="50%">

The basic idea of ​​the algorithm:
1. Let the unit vector of the navigation direction be $\vec{d}$;
2. Select the control rect border from the center of the control along the direction  $\vec{d}$, that is, line segment $AB$ shown in the figure, and find the midpoint $C$ of the line segment;
3. Use the line AB ($`Line(AB)`$) to clip other controls. Keep the rect on the side of $Line(AB)$ along direction $\vec{d}$.
4. For any point $P$ that meets the requirements of step 3 (i.e., the point on the right side of $`Line(AB)`$ in the figure), we can find a circle passing through points $A$, $B$, and $P$, and let its center be $O$. It should be noted that the center $O$ is on the perpendicular bisector of line segment $AB$.
5. We can use the following scoring formula:

$$
\begin{align}
score(P) = -\vec{d} \cdot  \vec{CO}
\end{align}
$$

@rsubtil Thanks for making the proposal. Some minor notes:

It was @AdriaandeJongh who originally proposed using the "balloon" algorithm in Godot. I entered the discussion later with clarifications, input and suggestions.

The variation of the balloon algorithm used in Unity is the "center" one. The "edge" variation differs enough that I would not go so far as to say that it's battle-tested, as it has different characteristics and edge cases than the center variation. Our preliminary investigations makes the edge variation seem superior for most cases, but that doesn't mean that the "battle-tested" trait necessarily caries over.

@runevision thanks for the feedback! I misunderstood which algorithm was currently used by Unity. I've tweaked the proposal:
- Clarified the algorithm was developed both with you and Adriaan
- Reworded the detail between center and edge approaches, and clarify this proposed algorithm is not actually what Unity uses.
